---
import ToolPage from '../../components/tools/ToolPage.astro';
import { pct, pctInput, money, whole } from '../../tools/format';
import {
  compareRules,
  constantRealSurvival,
  CUT_LINE,
  SWR_RATES,
  SWR_YEARS,
} from '../../tools/engine/withdrawal';

const m = (x: number) => money(x, 'USD');
// The article's market model, computed by the calculator's own engine code at build time.
const ex = {
  balance: 1_000_000,
  rate: 4,
  years: 30,
  method: 'parametric',
  mean: 5,
  vol: 12,
  stocks: 60,
  trials: '2000',
  seed: 2026,
  tax: 0,
  timing: 'start',
  cur: 'USD',
} as const;
const stats = await compareRules(ex);
const rows = stats.map((s) => [
  s.name,
  m(s.year1),
  m(s.medianMid),
  m(s.p10Last),
  pct(s.belowLine, 1),
  pct(s.medianLargestCut, 1),
  pct(s.shortAny, 1),
]);
const constant = stats.find((s) => s.id === 'constant')!;
const guardrails = stats.find((s) => s.id === 'guardrails')!;
const percentRule = stats.find((s) => s.id === 'percent')!;

// Same engine path and the same inputs, so the 4% / 30-year cell matches the worked example.
const survival = await constantRealSurvival(ex);
const cell = (rate: number, years: number) => {
  const found = survival.find((c) => c.rate === rate && c.years === years);
  if (!found) throw new Error(`no survival cell for ${rate}% over ${years} years`);
  return found;
};
const fourThirty = cell(ex.rate, ex.years);
if (Math.abs(fourThirty.lasted - (1 - constant.shortAny)) > 1e-12)
  throw new Error('safe-withdrawal table disagrees with the constant-real row of the worked example');
const y30 = cell(4, 30);
const y40 = cell(4, 40);
const y50 = cell(4, 50);
const low50 = cell(3, 50);
const high50 = cell(5, 50);

const ext = { rel: 'noopener noreferrer' };
const bengen =
  'https://www.financialplanningassociation.org/sites/default/files/2021-04/MAR04%20Determining%20Withdrawal%20Rates%20Using%20Historical%20Data.pdf';
const trinity =
  'https://www.aaii.com/files/pdf/6794_retirement-savings-choosing-a-withdrawal-rate-that-is-sustainable.pdf';
const portfolioCharts = 'https://portfoliocharts.com/charts/withdrawal-rates/';
const guyton2004 =
  'https://www.financialplanningassociation.org/sites/default/files/2020-05/6%20Decision%20Rules%20and%20Portfolio%20management%20for%20Retirees%20Is%20Safe%20Initial%20Withdrawal%20Rate%20Too%20Safe.pdf';
const guyton2006 =
  'https://www.financialplanningassociation.org/sites/default/files/2021-11/2006%20-%20Guyton%20and%20Klinger%20-%20Decision%20Rules%20and%20SWR%20(1).PDF';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is 4% still a safe withdrawal rate?',
    a: `<p>On this page’s model, a constant-real 4% paid every year in ${pct(y30.lasted, 1)} of the 30-year simulations, ${pct(y40.lasted, 1)} of the 40-year simulations and ${pct(y50.lasted, 1)} of the 50-year simulations. Bengen’s 4% was a reading of US history over a retirement of at least 30 years, not a guarantee. Cooley, Hubbard and Walz called 3% and 4% exceedingly conservative for stock-dominated portfolios, and their longest payout was 30 years. Whether 4% is safe is the chance you accept on the horizon you have. The assumptions are stated with the table on this page.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What withdrawal rate is safe for a 40- or 50-year retirement?',
    a: `<p>No rate in the table lasted every simulation. Over 40 years, a constant-real 3% paid every year in ${pct(cell(3, 40).lasted, 1)} of simulations, 4% in ${pct(cell(4, 40).lasted, 1)} and 5% in ${pct(cell(5, 40).lasted, 1)}. Over 50 years those figures are ${pct(cell(3, 50).lasted, 1)}, ${pct(cell(4, 50).lasted, 1)} and ${pct(cell(5, 50).lasted, 1)}. Each rate lasts less often as the horizon grows. The studies behind 4% stopped at about 30 years; the table has 3.5% and 4.5% as well.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a perpetual withdrawal rate?',
    a: `<p>A rate meant to last indefinitely, rather than for a fixed 30 years. <a href="${portfolioCharts}">Portfolio Charts</a> defines its perpetual withdrawal rate as the rate that, in the worst historical period of a chosen length, still left at least the original inflation-adjusted principal. “Lasted” in the table means every year was paid, which can still leave the portfolio near zero. At 50 years, the longest horizon here, a constant-real 4% still held at least the starting amount in ${pct(y50.preserved, 1)} of simulations. The table reports those chances. It does not solve for one rate that would have kept the principal in every case, and 50 years is still a finite horizon.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the best retirement withdrawal strategy?',
    a: '<p>There is no best rule, only a trade-off. Constant real withdrawals keep income steady and risk running out; a fixed percentage never runs out and lets income move with the market; guardrails and VPW sit between. Which suits you depends on how much of your spending is fixed, what other income you have and whether you want to leave money behind.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Guyton-Klinger the same as guardrails?',
    a: '<p>Guyton and Klinger described two of their rules as guardrails, and the name is now used for any rule that changes spending when a trigger is crossed. The full planner also has risk-based guardrails that react to the plan’s chance of success instead of its withdrawal rate.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do Guyton-Klinger guardrails change the withdrawal?',
    a: `<p>They start like the constant-real rule. Spending then skips its inflation raise after a losing year when the withdrawal rate is already above the rate you started with. If the rate rises more than 20% above that initial rate, spending is cut by 10% (not in the last 15 years); if it falls more than 20% below, spending is raised by 10%. In this page’s worked example the median largest one-year cut is ${pct(guardrails.medianLargestCut, 1)}, and a year went unpaid in ${pct(guardrails.shortAny, 1)} of simulations, against ${pct(constant.shortAny, 1)} for constant real withdrawals of the same 4%. Here the band and the adjustment are fixed at those 2006 figures; the <a href="/tools/guardrails-retirement-calculator/">guardrails calculator</a> lets you change them and shows the portfolio values at which spending would be cut or raised.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Can a percentage-of-portfolio rule fail?',
    a: '<p>It cannot reach zero, because each withdrawal is a share of what is left. But income can fall to a level that does not cover your spending, which is a failure of a different kind; the column for income below 75% of year 1 measures that.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do the numbers differ from the blog article?',
    a: '<p>The article’s table came from an independent script with its own random draws, start-of-year withdrawals and a simpler VPW. This calculator runs the planner’s engine. The pattern is the same; individual figures differ by a point or two, and VPW starts higher because the engine’s VPW assumes a higher return.</p>',
  },
];
---

<ToolPage
  id="withdrawal"
  heading="Retirement withdrawal calculator: safe withdrawal rate, the 4% rule and four strategies"
  lede="A safe withdrawal rate and the 4% rule, set beside constant real withdrawals, a fixed percentage, Guyton-Klinger guardrails and VPW. The same simulated markets show where each rule puts its risk: in your income, or in running out. It runs the Glideworth planning engine in your browser; nothing you type is sent anywhere."
  faq={faq}
  adds="The planner runs these rules, and more, on your whole plan with other income and tax."
  related={[
    { href: '/features/withdrawal-strategies/', label: 'Withdrawal strategies in the planner' },
    { href: '/tools/guardrails-retirement-calculator/', label: 'Guardrails retirement calculator' },
    { href: '/docs/planning/solvers-and-guardrails/', label: 'Guide: solvers and guardrails' },
    { href: '/blog/what-is-your-fire-number/', label: 'Where the 4% rule came from' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>The four rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Constant real (“the 4% rule”).</strong> Year 1 takes your initial rate of the starting portfolio; every
      later year takes the same amount raised by inflation. The portfolio’s value is never consulted. This is the rule
      inside William Bengen’s <a {...ext} href={bengen}>1994 study</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fixed percentage.</strong> Every year takes the same percentage of the current portfolio. It cannot run
      out, and income moves with the portfolio: a 25% fall in value is a 25% fall in next year’s income.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Guyton-Klinger guardrails</strong>, from Guyton’s <a {...ext} href={guyton2004}>2004</a> and Guyton and
      Klinger’s <a {...ext} href={guyton2006}>2006</a> papers. Withdrawals rise with inflation, except after a year with
      a negative return when the current rate is above the initial rate. If the current rate is more than 20% above the
      initial rate, spending is cut by 10% (not in the last 15 years); if it is more than 20% below, spending is raised
      by 10%.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>VPW (variable percentage withdrawal).</strong> Each year takes the payment that would spend the portfolio
      exactly over the years left at an assumed return, so the percentage rises as the horizon shortens. The engine
      assumes 5% a year after inflation for stocks and 1.9% for bonds, weighted by the mix (the random-returns model
      counts as all stocks). The community version is described on the <a {...ext}
        href="https://www.finiki.org/wiki/Variable_percentage_withdrawal">finiki VPW page</a
      >.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="safe-withdrawal-rate">What a safe withdrawal rate is</h2>
  <p>
    A safe withdrawal rate is the share of the portfolio taken in the first year, then raised with inflation, that still
    has a chance you accept of lasting the retirement. The chance is part of the answer: a rate is safe only relative to
    a horizon and a chance you choose. The studies behind the label tested the constant-real rule, the first of the four
    above.
  </p>
  <p>
    William Bengen’s <a {...ext} href={bengen}>“Determining Withdrawal Rates Using Historical Data”</a> (<em
      >Journal of Financial Planning</em
    >, October 1994) tested that rule on US history. For a mix of stocks and intermediate-term Treasuries, and a
    retirement of at least 30 years, he read a first-year withdrawal of 4% as one that should be safe. The
    <a href="/blog/what-is-your-fire-number/">FIRE number article</a> sets out the limits of that reading: one country’s
    history, a horizon of about 30 years, a stock-heavy mix, spending that never changes, and in the main case no costs
    or tax.
  </p>
  <p>
    Philip Cooley, Carl Hubbard and Daniel Walz, <a {...ext} href={trinity}
      >“Retirement Savings: Choosing a Withdrawal Rate That Is Sustainable”</a
    > (<em>AAII Journal</em>, February 1998), measured a portfolio success rate: the share of overlapping historical
    payout periods in which the ending value was still above zero. They used US stock and bond returns from 1926 to
    1995, withdrawal rates from 3% to 12%, and payout periods of 15, 20, 25 and 30 years, and they did not adjust for
    taxes or costs. For stock-dominated portfolios they described 3% and 4% as exceedingly conservative. One of their
    conclusions is that early retirees who expect a long payout should plan on a lower rate. Their longest payout was
    30 years.
  </p>

  <h2>How often a constant-real withdrawal lasted</h2>
  <p>
    Computed by this page’s own code when the page was built, for the constant-real rule only. Starting portfolio
    {m(ex.balance)}. Each year’s real return is drawn independently from a log-normal distribution with an arithmetic
    average of {pctInput(ex.mean)} and a standard deviation of {pctInput(ex.vol)}. Inflation is held at 0, so the return
    is already after inflation and a constant-real withdrawal is a constant amount in today’s money. {whole(
      Number(ex.trials),
    )}{' '}
    simulations, seed {ex.seed}, withdrawals at the start of each year, no tax, no fees and no other income. The same
    assumptions and seed as the worked example below, so the 4% row at 30 years matches that example’s constant-real
    result: every year was paid in {pct(fourThirty.lasted, 1)} of simulations, and a year went unpaid in {pct(
      constant.shortAny,
      1,
    )}.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="Table, scrolls sideways if it does not fit">
    <table>
      <caption>
        Share of simulations in which the constant-real rule paid every year in full
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Initial rate</th>
          {SWR_YEARS.map((years) => <th>{years} years</th>)}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          SWR_RATES.map((rate) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{pctInput(rate)}</td>
              {SWR_YEARS.map((years) => (
                <td>{pct(cell(rate, years).lasted, 1)}</td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The 30-year column is the horizon of the studies above. The 40- and 50-year columns are there because a retirement
    that starts in someone’s 40s or 50s can be longer than 30 years. The 4% rate over 30 years paid every year in {pct(
      y30.lasted,
      1,
    )} of these simulations. Each cell is one sample of {whole(Number(ex.trials))} seeded draws, so another seed would move it by
    about a percentage point or two. The calculator above follows the inputs you type, and it can switch the
    market model to US history from 1928 to 2025. The table is fixed when the page is built.
  </p>

  <h2 id="perpetual-withdrawal-rate">What a perpetual withdrawal rate means here</h2>
  <p>
    A perpetual withdrawal rate is a rate meant to last indefinitely, rather than for a fixed number of years.
    <a {...ext} href={portfolioCharts}>Portfolio Charts</a> defines its perpetual withdrawal rate as the rate that, over
    the worst historical retirement of a chosen length, still left at least the original inflation-adjusted principal,
    and its safe withdrawal rate as the rate that ended that worst case at zero. The table on this page is computed by
    this site’s engine under the assumptions stated above.
  </p>
  <p>
    “Lasted” in the table means every withdrawal was paid. A simulation can finish near zero and still count. That
    would leave nothing for the years after the horizon. The figure is a share of random simulations, while the
    Portfolio Charts reading is the single worst historical case. The 50-year column is the longest horizon here.
  </p>
  <p>
    On this model, with the 5% average return above, a constant-real 3% over 50 years paid every year in {pct(
      low50.lasted,
      1,
    )} of simulations and still
    held at least the starting {m(ex.balance)}, in today’s money, in {pct(low50.preserved, 1)} of them. The median
    amount left was {m(low50.medianEnd)}. At 4% over 50 years, every year was paid in {pct(y50.lasted, 1)} and the
    starting amount was still there in {pct(y50.preserved, 1)}, with a median of {m(y50.medianEnd)} left. At 5% over 50
    years, every year was paid in {pct(high50.lasted, 1)} and the starting amount was still there in {pct(
      high50.preserved,
      1,
    )}. Holding the starting amount is the stricter reading, and a 50-year horizon is still finite. A fixed percentage
    of the portfolio cannot reach zero. That is a different promise: the income moves with the balance, and the table
    above is the constant-real rule only.
  </p>

  <h2 id="guardrails">Guyton-Klinger guardrails</h2>
  <p>
    This calculator is the Guyton guardrail calculator. The rule uses the 20% band and the 10% adjustment from the <a
      {...ext}
      href={guyton2006}>2006 paper</a
    >. The form on this page leaves both fixed. The full planner can change them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The guardrails react to the withdrawal rate. After a year whose return was negative, there is no inflation raise
    when the rate is already above the rate you started with, and there is no make-up later. When the current rate is
    more than 20% above the initial rate, spending is cut by 10%, except in the last 15 years of the plan. When it is
    more than 20% below, spending is raised by 10%. In the worked example (4% to start, 30 years, the market model
    above) the median largest one-year cut is {pct(guardrails.medianLargestCut, 1)} under the guardrails, {pct(
      constant.medianLargestCut,
      1,
    )} under constant real withdrawals and {pct(percentRule.medianLargestCut, 1)} under a fixed percentage. A year went
    unpaid in {pct(guardrails.shortAny, 1)} of the guardrail simulations, against {pct(constant.shortAny, 1)} for
    constant real withdrawals of the same initial amount. The rule caps the size of a cut. The chance of a year going
    unpaid remains.
  </p>

  <h2>How the calculator works</h2>
  <p>
    Each rule is set as the plan’s withdrawal strategy in the engine and run through the same Monte Carlo simulations:
    the same seed, so every rule sees exactly the same sequences of returns. “Income” is what the rule actually paid
    each year, in today’s money. The calculator then reads, for each rule, the first-year income, the middle and bad-luck
    income later on, how often income fell below {pct(CUT_LINE, 0)} of its first year, the typical largest one-year cut,
    and how often the rule could not pay its own amount.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two market models are offered: random real returns drawn independently each year from a log-normal distribution
    (average 5%, volatility 12% by default, the model in the blog article and in the table above), or US history from
    1928 to 2025 with random start years and a stock and bond mix.
  </p>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    Computed by the calculator’s own code when this page was built: {m(1_000_000)}, a 4% initial rate, 30 years,
    random real returns averaging 5% with 12% volatility, 2,000 simulations with seed 2026, withdrawals at the start of
    each year, no tax.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="Table, scrolls sideways if it does not fit">
  <table>
    <caption>Four rules on the same 2,000 simulated retirements (today’s money)</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rule</th><th>Year-1 income</th><th>Median, year 15</th><th>10th percentile, year 30</th><th
          >Income ever below 75% of year 1</th
        ><th>Median largest one-year cut</th><th>A year not paid in full</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>{rows.map((r) => <tr>{r.map((c) => <td>{c}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The pattern is the one the <a href="/blog/withdrawal-strategies-compared/">withdrawal strategies article</a> found
    with its own script: constant real income never moves until it fails (in {pct(constant.shortAny, 1)} of these
    simulations a year went unpaid, which is {pct(1 - constant.shortAny, 1)} in which every year was paid, the 4% /
    30-year cell above); a fixed percentage never runs out but often cuts income; guardrails cap the typical
    cut at 10%; VPW starts higher and spends the portfolio down. For VPW, “a year not paid in full” is mostly its final
    year: the engine asks for the whole remaining balance grown at the assumed return, and a final year below that
    return leaves a small part unpaid.
  </p>

  <h2>What this assumes and leaves out</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The results depend on the market model: a different return, volatility or horizon changes every row.
      Independent yearly returns understate runs of bad years; the history model keeps them but has few independent
      sequences. The safe-withdrawal table uses only the random-returns model stated beside it.
    </li>
    <li>One portfolio, rebalanced yearly; no fees; no tax unless you set the flat rate.</li>
    <li>No pension or other income. A floor of guaranteed income changes which rule suits you most.</li>
    <li>
      The Guyton-Klinger rules are applied as the engine implements them (one asset mix, so the portfolio management
      rule does not apply). The 20% guardrail and the 10% adjustment are fixed on this page.
    </li>
    <li>
      History data: Aswath Damodaran, <em>Historical Returns on Stocks, Bonds and Bills</em> (NYU Stern), and US Bureau
      of Labor Statistics CPI-U.
    </li>
  </ul>
</ToolPage>
